docs/debate/codex_r1.md:1:Try & Buy as-is probably does not place top-3. It is coherent, shoppable, and track-aligned, but it reads like the expected answer: upload selfie, try outfit, get matching makeup, add to cart. Worse, Kostuma already owns the “outfit plus matching makeup basket” demo shape, just with Halloween IP. Try & Buy is more commercially credible, but judges watching 1–3 minutes may see “regional eCommerce wrapper around standard VTO,” not “from another planet.” The hijab gap also hurts: if modest fashion is the differentiator, the missing API support sits exactly where the promise lives.
docs/debate/codex_r1.md:7:Non-obvious mechanism: Not “AI recommends products,” but “commerce as identity simulation.” The app sells complete social contexts. A look is not a SKU bundle; it is a persona-state with visual proof.
docs/debate/codex_r1.md:9:YouCam chain: Clothes VTO v4 for each outfit, Makeup VTO for each makeup map, Earrings/Necklace VTO for accessories. Optional Hair Color for one bolder persona. Cost: 3 outfits x 2 = 6, 3 makeup x 1 = 3, 3 jewelry x 1–2 = 3–6. Total ~12–15 units/session.
docs/debate/codex_r1.md:11:Commerce impact: Useful for MENA shoppers buying for high-context events: engagement visits, workwear, family dinners, Eid gatherings. The problem is not “what looks nice?” but “which version of me fits this situation without being too much or too little?” This can sell bundles credibly.
docs/debate/codex_r1.md:13:Riskiest assumption: Sequential VTO outputs stay visually clean when makeup/jewelry are applied after clothes. De-risk: test 3 chains on 2 selfies using Clothes + Makeup + Earrings/Necklace, ≤12 units.
docs/debate/codex_r1.md:21:Non-obvious mechanism: It turns VTO into a color-science proof, not a stylist. The “wow” is the app visibly locking a palette from face undertone plus garment color, then applying makeup and accessories that mathematically harmonize.
docs/debate/codex_r1.md:23:YouCam chain: Facial Color Tones for undertone = 20 units, Clothes VTO = 2, Makeup VTO with generated shades = 1, Earrings/Necklace = 1–2. Optional Fitzpatrick = 2 if Facial Color Tones is too expensive. Total: premium session ~24–25 units; cheap demo mode ~5–7 units.
docs/debate/codex_r1.md:25:Commerce impact: Big for beauty + fashion baskets, especially where foundation/lipstick mismatch and event outfits drive returns or hesitation. Audience: shoppers buying occasionwear who need makeup shades that survive different lighting and skin undertones.
docs/debate/codex_r1.md:27:Riskiest assumption: Facial Color Tones output is available and useful enough for visible palette logic. De-risk: run Facial Color Tones on one photo and compare generated warm/cool makeup results, ≤20–24 units depending one success.
docs/debate/codex_r1.md:35:Non-obvious mechanism: It reframes VTO from aspiration to regret prevention. Most shopping apps say “you’ll look great.” This one says “here are the three visual reasons you may hate this after delivery,” then uses VTO to resolve them.
docs/debate/codex_r1.md:37:YouCam chain: Clothes VTO for base outfit = 2, Makeup VTO for current/better makeup = 1–2, Necklace/Earrings/Shoes/Bag VTO alternatives = 2–6 depending categories. Total ~7–10 units/session.
docs/debate/codex_r1.md:39:Commerce impact: Extremely credible for eCommerce: reduce returns, increase basket confidence, sell substitutes. Audience: online fashion marketplaces, especially occasionwear and beauty bundles where items are bought separately and only clash after delivery.
docs/debate/codex_r1.md:41:Riskiest assumption: Shoe/bag/jewelry VTO outputs work well enough from one user photo to make “regret fixes” visible. De-risk: test one outfit plus two accessory swaps and one makeup swap, ≤8 units.
docs/debate/codex_r1.md:49:Mirror Passport is more glamorous, but slightly more “styling app.” Shade-Lock is elegant, but Facial Color Tones is expensive and risks becoming color theory homework. Return-Proof Cart is weird enough to feel new, practical enough for judges to believe, and buildable by one developer in three focused days.
