docs/debate/codex_r4.log:131:**Verdict:** yes, the seller-owned registry makes Drape stronger, but only if it is framed as the reliability layer, not as “we already work everywhere.” It turns Drape from a clever VTO demo into a network: sellers contribute clean assets once, then buyers can discover the same product from any storefront.
docs/debate/codex_r4.log:133:The critical risks are real. Cold start means most extension clicks will not match anything at first. Seller incentive is also not automatic: sellers need a reason to upload assets, such as higher conversion, palette-personalized bundles, analytics, or “Drape-ready” placement. Payment should probably start seller-side, not buyer-side: free demo tier, then subscription or per-active-SKU once Drape drives measurable engagement. Wrong or fake links are dangerous because they could send buyers to the wrong product or counterfeit listing. Marketplace ToS also matters: Drape should read public page metadata conservatively, avoid scraping behind login, avoid copying marketplace UI, and treat Amazon/Noon/etc. as linked sources rather than integration partners.
docs/debate/codex_r4.log:139:1. Normalize URL: strip tracking params, force canonical host/path, resolve redirects where allowed, and compare against seller-submitted listing links.
docs/debate/codex_r4.log:141:3. Exact registry match: seller product has that URL, canonical URL, ASIN, SKU, GTIN, or store handle.
docs/debate/codex_r4.log:142:4. Strong fuzzy match: same seller/brand plus high title similarity plus image similarity against registered clean product or colourway images.
docs/debate/codex_r4.log:145:To stop wrong matches: require exact ID or seller-verified URL for “Drape-ready” status; use confidence tiers; show product name, brand, colour, and seller before try-on; let sellers verify domains/listings; keep an abuse report button; log match decisions; never merge products automatically across sellers.
docs/debate/codex_r4.log:155:Roadmap: seller verification, Shopify app, Amazon-specific adapters, bulk import, GTIN databases, analytics dashboard, marketplace compliance review, paid seller plans, fraud detection.
docs/debate/codex_r4.log:157:**3-minute video:** use two fake stores on custom domains. First, seller uploads one product with three colourways and attaches two store links. Second, buyer visits Store A, clicks Drape, instant match, gets colour drape, try-on, makeup-on-selfie, jewelry bundle, cart. Third, buyer visits Store B with the same product, clicks Drape, same registry product appears. Fourth, show a no-match page using picker fallback. Close with the point: sellers prepare once; buyers get reliable try-on anywhere.
docs/debate/codex_r4.log:159:**Extra improvement:** add a seller “Drape Link Health” dashboard. It flags broken links, duplicate marketplace listings, missing colourways, suspicious mismatches, and which external listings are driving try-ons. That makes the registry valuable even before massive buyer adoption: sellers get a living catalogue control panel, not just another upload form.
