docs/debate/codex_r3.md:1:1. **Seller-side verdict: strengthen, but only if it becomes the core reliability layer.** Drape should not depend on messy buyer-scraped product photos. Sellers prepare each SKU before buyers arrive: upload clean front-facing product/colorway shots, choose category (`upper_body`, `full_body`, `outer`, etc.), tag color family, undertone fit, metal pairing, fabric finish, and optionally approve seller-time test renders on a standard model.
docs/debate/codex_r3.md:3:Seller-time YouCam calls: optional Clothes VTO validation on a stock/persona body for each colorway, plus maybe pre-rendered demo assets for judges. Seller pays these units because it is catalog onboarding, like product photography.
docs/debate/codex_r3.md:5:Buyer-time YouCam calls: tone read once, then Clothes VTO on the buyer’s body, Makeup VTO on face or full output if supported, jewelry VTO if used. Buyer, retailer, or platform pays depending on business model, but the app should present it as retailer-funded confidence, not a user tax.
docs/debate/codex_r3.md:9:2. **Extension verdict: good as a buyer acquisition surface, bad as the main demo dependency.** One click works like this: content script detects product images from DOM/OpenGraph/schema.org, picks the largest visible product-like image, extracts title/price/color text, guesses category from page text and image aspect, then sends candidate image URL + page metadata to backend. Backend checks whether the merchant/SKU is already seller-prepared. If yes, launch Drape with reliable assets. If no, show “needs seller-ready image” or a limited “save to Drape” flow.
docs/debate/codex_r3.md:19:- Buyer app/web: selfie, tone read, drape reveal, seller-prepared catalog, full-body try-on, match-my-face bundle, mock checkout.
docs/debate/codex_r3.md:20:- Buyer extension: one-click bridge from generic product page into Drape, best when SKU is seller-prepared.
docs/debate/codex_r3.md:25:- Backend stores cached palette profile, result images, product metadata, and unit budget state.
docs/debate/codex_r3.md:28:- Seller onboarding: optional Clothes VTO validation, 2u per colorway/persona test, owner-approved only.
docs/debate/codex_r3.md:29:- Buyer demo mode: Fitzpatrick 2u or cached profile, Clothes VTO 2u, Makeup VTO 1u, Earrings/Necklace 1u. Around 6u live demo.
docs/debate/codex_r3.md:30:- Premium mode: Facial Color Tones 20u once, then 4–5u per new look.
docs/debate/codex_r3.md:31:- Extension: 0u until it finds a seller-prepared SKU or the buyer explicitly starts try-on.
docs/debate/codex_r3.md:41:- End with architecture shot: seller prep makes buyer VTO cheap, reliable, and extension-ready.
docs/debate/codex_r3.md:43:4. **MVP cut by Nov 2.** Build seller console-lite, backend proxy/cache/budget guard, Flutter buyer flow, synthetic catalog, mock checkout, and load-unpacked Chrome extension for a local/mock product page. Use cached judge persona so testing until Nov 20 costs nothing. Build live buyer mode behind owner-approved caps.
docs/debate/codex_r3.md:45:Roadmap only: real Amazon support, public Chrome Web Store review, arbitrary merchant scraping, seller billing, automated garment QA, full marketplace integrations, and premium Color Tones for every user.
docs/debate/codex_r3.md:47:5. **Biggest single risk:** whether real clean colorway product shots preserve garment shape consistently in Clothes VTO. If colorway A renders as a ruffle and colorway B as a cape, seller prep does not save the product.
docs/debate/codex_r3.md:49:Cheapest test: owner-approved 6 units. Use one person photo and three real clean colorway images of the same garment. Run Clothes VTO x3. If silhouette stays stable, Drape is viable. If not, MVP must show one approved colorway per SKU and keep color comparison in the face-drape overlay only.
docs/debate/r5b.md:4:- **Codex, `codex_r5.md`: Merchandising Radar.** A seller dashboard of measured shopper palettes, rejected and rescued colours,
docs/debate/r5b.md:5:  and "the colour you're missing", built from Drape's own events; 0 extra units.
docs/debate/r5b.md:6:- **Fable, `fable_r5.md`: "Ask for it in my colour".** When no colourway suits the buyer, YouCam Fabric recolours the seller's
docs/debate/r5b.md:7:  shot into her palette colour, Clothes VTO renders it on her body ("not sold yet"), and she asks the store to make it; the
docs/debate/r5b.md:8:  console shows the requests and can add the colour as a draft colourway. About 16 units to build, 2–4 per request.
docs/debate/codex_r2.md:3:The biggest weakness is unit cost. Facial Color Tones is 20 units before the app has even shown a shoppable transformation. Then Drape proposes 4 Clothes VTO calls, so the core demo is ~28–30 units/session. With a 1,000-unit budget, that leaves little room for failed calls, testing, judges, friends, retries, and video production. For a one-dev hackathon build, the winning concept should not depend on the most expensive API being both accurate and visibly meaningful on camera.
docs/debate/codex_r2.md:7:Third: Drape risks being judged as “seasonal color analysis + VTO,” which is already a known consumer pattern. It is differentiated from Try & Buy, yes, but not necessarily “another planet.” The hijab workaround is clever, but a recolored drape overlay is not the same as true hijab try-on; if presented too boldly, it exposes the API gap again.
docs/debate/codex_r2.md:15:Mechanism: Combine Drape’s color logic with my regret-prevention framing. Do not sell “AI stylist.” Sell “visual return insurance.” The system generates a bad cart and a fixed cart, using face tone/color rules plus VTO proof. The alien part is that the mirror argues against the purchase before selling the better one.
docs/debate/codex_r2.md:18:Cheap judge/demo mode: Fitzpatrick skin type 2 + Clothes VTO 2 + Makeup VTO 1 + Necklace/Earrings 1–2 = **6–7 units/session**.  
docs/debate/codex_r2.md:19:Premium optional mode: Facial Color Tones 20 once per user, then Clothes 2 + Makeup 1 + Jewelry 1–2 = **24–25 units first session**, **4–5 units after cached tone**.
docs/debate/codex_r2.md:23:5–15s: Clothes VTO renders it. The app stamps: “Return risk: color drains warmth from face.”  
docs/debate/codex_r2.md:24:15–25s: It tries the common mistake: cool pink lipstick + tiny silver necklace. Makeup/Jewelry VTO shows the clash.  
docs/debate/codex_r2.md:27:50–60s: Add full repaired bundle to cart. Tagline: “Don’t recommend products. Prevent returns.”
docs/debate/codex_r2.md:29:Cheapest de-risk: **7 units**. Run Fitzpatrick (2), one Clothes VTO (2), one Makeup VTO (1), one Earrings/Necklace VTO (1–2). If the visual difference reads on a phone recording, the concept works. Only spend the 20-unit Facial Color Tones call after that.
