docs/debate/codex_r3.log:266:- **Video wow.** The clash is a caption, not a picture. Clothes VTO will not render a neckline-necklace clash a judge perceives in two seconds; the viewer sees two renders and a label saying which is bad. The wow lives in the copy.
docs/debate/codex_r3.log:274:- **Budget pattern.** "One premium analysis, then many low-cost try-ons", with Fitzpatrick (2) as demo-mode fallback for Facial Color Tones (20), beats my flat 30 units per session. Judges re-testing until Nov 20 need the cheap mode.
docs/debate/codex_r3.log:283:**Mechanism:** the app may only veto what a measurement of the face can justify. Facial Color Tones gives undertone; a deterministic draping table maps it to garment colourways, foundation and lip shade, and metal (warm: gold, cool: silver). The veto is shown, not captioned: the rejected colourway is rendered on your body beside the approved one. No opinions on taste, neckline or formality, ever; that restraint is what makes it credible and not a stylist. Hijab colour is the same drape overlay around the face, no hijab API needed.
docs/debate/codex_r3.log:285:**API chain, per session:** Facial Color Tones 20 (once, cached) → local HSL recolour of garment image (0) → Clothes VTO x3: two approved, one vetoed (6) → Makeup VTO foundation + lip (2) → Earrings, metal by undertone (1). Premium session 29; demo mode with Fitzpatrick 11. After ~80 units of build and de-risk, roughly 25 premium plus 20 cheap sessions remain.
docs/debate/codex_r3.log:289:- 6–16: undertone pill appears; five drapes sweep in, grey versus glow (overlay, zero units).
docs/debate/codex_r3.log:295:**Cheapest de-risk:** 3 Clothes VTO calls on hue-shifted garment images, 6 units; that is the single load-bearing assumption. If it holds, Fitzpatrick (2) + Makeup (1), then one Color Tones call (20).
